window.docContents[0].resolve({"/#Jakarta-Persistence--3___-Entity-Operations--3___6___-Entity-Instance-Lifecycle--3___6___8___-Evicting-an-Entity-Instance-from-the-Persistence-Context":{"contents":"A managed entity instance is removed from the persistence context when it is passed as an argument to the detach() method of EntityManager or when the detach operation cascades from another instance to the managed instance.\n\nLet e be an instance of an entity class. If e is passed as an argument to the detach() method of EntityManager, or if the detach operation is applied to e via cascade:\n\n* if e is a managed entity, it is removed from the persistence context and becomes detached;* if e is a new or detached entity, it is ignored by the detach operation;* if e is a removed entity, it becomes detached, and the scheduled removal of the record represented by e is cancelled.* If e is managed or removed, the detach operation is also applied to every entity t referenced by a relationship owned by e and annotated with the cascade element value cascade=DETACH or cascade=ALL or mapped as such by the equivalent XML descriptor element.\n\nPortable applications should not pass removed entities that have been detached from the persistence context to methods of EntityManager.\n\nEntities which referenced e before detachment continue to reference e after detachment.\n\nNotePending modifications to the state of the entity, including removal of the entity, are never synchronized to the database after the entity is evicted from the persistence context. If such pending modifications exist, an application must call the flush() method before calling detach() if portable behavior is required. A portable application must not assume that pending modifications have not been written to the database if the flush() method was not called before detach(). More information about synchronization may be found above in Section 3.6.6.\n\n\n\nFormalized SpecificationDetach requires a created instance. It changes a\nmanaged or removed instance to detached and leaves a new or already\ndetached instance unchanged. Its persistent identity is preserved in every case.action Detach (e : entity) {\n  require created e\n  if e_phase e = managed ∨ e_phase e = removed then\n    e_phase e := detached\n}\nAfter detachment, both belongsToContext e and pendingRemoval e\nare false.\nThis represents leaving the context and, for a removed instance, cancelling\npending removal. We do not record whether an instance was removed before it\nwas detached, so the model does not enforce the specification's restriction on\npassing such instances to later EntityManager calls.\n\n\n\n","context":"Jakarta Persistence\u00093. Entity Operations\u00093.6. Entity Instance Lifecycle","header":"3.6.8. Evicting an Entity Instance from the Persistence Context","id":"/#Jakarta-Persistence--3___-Entity-Operations--3___6___-Entity-Instance-Lifecycle--3___6___8___-Evicting-an-Entity-Instance-from-the-Persistence-Context"}});